Super user rights are usually restricted to the root account. This means you
will have to log into a root shell to make any changes to your system. You could
also run commands through su, which does not cache credentials and requires you
to enter your password on each invocation. For this reason, most systems make
use of a helper application that allows regular users selected by root to run
commands with elevated privileges.

BSD's doas has been gaining traction for this purpose on Linux as it is simple
and easy to set up. Its feature set is by design very small and does not offer
much in terms of customization. The more established sudo falls on the other
side of the scale, offering more features than most home users will probably
ever need. There is also sudo-rs, a drop-in and memory safe replacement for sudo
written in Rust, which offers something of a middle ground. While it is a
reimplementation of the established original sudo, one of its goals is to
simplify and modernize the code base. Therefore, only a subset of the original
sudo's feature set is implemented.

When using doas, please keep in mind that a lot of the standard Arch Linux tools
still depend on the sudo command being available. sudo is part of base-devel and
therefore required for all user-built packages. If you want to completely
replace sudo with doas, additional steps are required. You would need to provide
a wrapper that translates sudo parameters into their doas equivalents (if they
exist). The AUR provides a user package for this purpose called
"doas-sudo-shim", which should serve as a good starting point.

If you decide to install either of these programs, you can then set up rules to
allow users belonging to a specific group to gain super-user privileges when
they invoke the program. For historical reasons, this group is usually named
"wheel" so that is also the name we will use in this script.